## User preferences

- When the user asks to “Review all open trails” and “keep looping until the trail is ready for approval” -> inventory the complete live portfolio, fix technical issues autonomously, and return only for a genuine human decision or blocker. [Task 1]
- When asked which trails are safe for agent approval -> report technical readiness and human approval separately; do not approve or merge higher-risk cache, namespace, semantic, or performance work merely because CI is green. [Task 1][Task 2]

## Reusable knowledge

- Start with `entire trail list --status open --limit 200 --json`. Final readiness requires every configured runner passed on the exact final SHA, `entire trail finding <trail> --freshness any --status open --json` with `Open: 0`, fresh CI/workflows, explicit ahead/behind and mergeability, resolved forge threads, and separately reported approval. [Task 1]
- The installed provider exposes `entire graph search`, not `entire graph query`; consult `entire agent-help` and `entire graph help`. A runner launch with `outcome: accepted` confirms only launch acceptance, so re-query all gates. [Task 1]
- Generated artifacts are real gates: regenerate benchmark hashes in `bench/memory/FAIR-CONFIG.md` via `runmeta.code_hashes()` and `NOTICES` via `scripts/gen-notices.sh`, then run `scripts/gen-notices.sh --check`. For stacked branches, resolve content plus rename/location conflicts and inspect every unmerged path with `git status --short`. [Task 1]
- Trail 243 final head `c564f910b6c82abf4ac3c94bb05e460b2622c267` / checkpoint `9f9558c19ca6` passed 34 technical checks, 4 workflows, 7 evaluations, zero findings, resolved threads, and Windows execution evidence (3,468 expected roots); it was technically ready but not agent-approved. [Task 2]
- Guard history refresh immediately before Git subprocesses when metadata can become unsafe; test namespace-rebound cache output against cold builds across syntax-only, fast, and full profiles. Trail 247 / PR 329 was the next candidate at capture, but required updating onto the merged Trail 243/main lineage before approval. [Task 2][Task 3]

## Failures and how to do differently

- A green runner launch or platform test does not prove final readiness: re-query results, CI, findings, mergeability, and the exact tested SHA. A generated parity/license failure must be fixed at its authoritative artifact. [Task 1]
- Initial Trail 243 testing let history refresh occur after metadata became unsafe -> recheck immediately before the subprocess and retain a failing-before/passing-after test. Do not call a bounded cache measurement a speedup when shared execution (`9.740s`) is slower than uncached (`2.375s`); disclose it and retain manual review. [Task 2]

