ies the caller's buffer, preserves short-write semantics, is reused rather than double-wrapped, and stays visible to terminal detection."}]}, {"title": "Provider enforcement and cache safety", "summary": "How the sem provider observes the deadline during parsing and relation building, and how it keeps truncated results out of the caches.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/sem/budget.go", "internal/sem/provider.go", "internal/sem/provider_parallel.go", "internal/sem/parser.go", "internal/sem/js_scopes.go", "internal/sem/python_scopes.go", "internal/sem/julia_bindings.go", "internal/sem/groovy.go", "internal/sem/yaml.go", "internal/sem/similarity.go", "internal/sem/health.go"], "title": "Budget gate and cooperative polling in parse and relation work", "description": "A new `budgetGate` reads the clock against the deadline directly, so expiry is observed even before the context timer fires. It also wraps the content reader so reads refuse once the budget is gone. Parse contexts are rooted in the caller's budget. Polling is added to the walk, parsers, relation producers, manifest resolvers, similarity, JS/Python scopes and several sort and reduce steps. Truncated builds are deterministic and skip the remaining relation work. Expiry of an opt-in budget is told apart from the caller's own deadline or cancellation, including ties."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/records_cache.go", "internal/sem/search_cache.go", "internal/sem/search_replay_policy.go", "internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "Truncated snapshots are never cached", "description": "Both cache writers refuse a budget-truncated snapshot: the record cache and the search/preindex snapshot cache, which returns `ErrTruncatedSnapshotNotCacheable`. Selective-search derivations key failure filtering on the walk and keep record-less partial failures. A truncated derivation keeps its header, and replay fails closed when the policy carries no caller context. The cache-filter path stays file-atomic and honors cancellation."}]}, {"title": "Git subprocess errors and test coverage", "summary": "Context errors from Git subprocesses are now reported accurately, and the branch adds a large regression suite.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/gitutil/git.go", "internal/gitutil/git_test.go", "internal/gitutil/run_context_error_test.go"], "title": "Context-aware Git errors", "description": "When a Git subprocess is killed because the caller's context ended, the error now wraps the context's error with `%w`. This keeps Git's argv and stderr in the message, and `errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded)` holds. `run()` previously returned the bare context error and dropped the argv and stderr text. The `cat-file --batch-command` probe and the `ls-tree` metadata paths no longer blame an old Git version when the cause was cancellation."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/tf142_budget_test.go", "internal/sem/tf142r8_budget_test.go", "internal/sem/tf142r17_selective_sort_budget_test.go", "internal/sem/tf142r22_selective_header_budget_test.go", "internal/sem/tf142r24_selective_failures_test.go", "internal/sem/provider_budget_test.go", "internal/sem/f06fix_budget_test.go"], "title": "Regression tests for budget gaps", "description": "About 25 `tf142r*` and `f06fix` test files pin individual budget and cancellation gaps found across review rounds. They cover relation and type producers, manifest resolvers, selective-search sort and header, reducers, and deterministic truncated output. Some existing tests were adjusted to the new signatures."}]}], "title": "Opt-in wall-clock budget (--max-seconds) and signal-safe cancellation", "summary": "Adds an opt-in `--max-seconds` ceiling to `snapshot`, `symbols` and `edges`. When it expires, the output is a truncated but self-describing stream that carries `E_ANALYSIS_BUDGET_EXCEEDED` and is never cached. The branch also makes cancellation work across the CLI, Git subprocesses and parse/relation loops, and adds conventional signal exit codes."}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T11:05:58.312Z"}
summary {"automation": "trail-summary", "resultType": "trail_summary", "data": {"markdown": "**Problem:** On `main`, `entire graph` has no time limit on parsing and relation building. A large repository or one pathological file (for example, deeply nested JavaScript functions) can run for a very long time. Cancelling with Ctrl-C also exits with status 1, the same as any other failure, so scripts can't tell a cancel from a real error.\n\n**Solution:** This branch adds an opt-in wall-clock ceiling, `--max-seconds`, to `snapshot`, `symbols`, and `edges`. The default is 0, which means no limit, so existing runs behave as before.\n\n- **Budget checks:** Parser, relation, and file-read loops check the clock and stop when the budget expires. The check uses the clock as well as the context timer, because the timer can fire late. The new `internal/sem/budget.go` holds this logic.\n- **Truncated output:** When the budget expires, the stream still finishes normally. It keeps the records produced so far, reports `E_ANALYSIS_BUDGET_EXCEEDED`, and sets health to `unsafe`.\n- **No caching of partial results:** A truncated snapshot is never written to the record cache or the search cache, so a partial graph can't be served to later unbudgeted queries.\n- **Rejected formats:** `--max-seconds` fails with `--format compact-ndjson` and `--format scip`. Those formats are defined as complete snapshots and cannot carry the truncation signal.\n- **Exit codes:** Ctrl-C and SIGTERM now exit with 130 and 143. Other errors still exit with 1.\n- **Git lookups:** Git subprocesses carry the caller's context, so cancellation reaches them.\n- **Limits:** The budget is cooperative, not a hard deadline. Source listing runs before the clock starts, and an in-flight parser or producer can finish after expiry. The docs say a process supervisor is still needed for strict limits.\n- **Contracts:** The diff does not touch `IdentityRevision` or symbol ID derivation, so stable IDs are unchanged. The parser changes are mostly threading a stop check through existing functions. Output is unchanged when no budget is set.\n- **Benchmarks:** No benchmark files changed. The updated docs record timings showing the relation phase is quadratic on deeply nested JavaScript (about 37 s for 500 nested functions, about 162 s for 1,000). The budget bounds that cost but does not remove it."}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T11:05:36.182Z"}
