trail-semantic-diff {"pages": [{"title": "Core BM25 IDF fix: use corpus counts when available", "summary": "The preselected candidate set is biased — files were chosen because they contain query terms, so every query term looks common within the selection. When the preselection pass can supply exact repository-wide document frequencies, BM25 IDF now uses those instead of the inflated sample counts, allowing rare identifying terms to outweigh common ones.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "Sample bias and the corpus IDF decision function", "description": "searchCorpusIDFStatistics chooses between sample and corpus document frequencies: when corpusFiles > 0 (the selection covered the whole corpus), corpus counts replace the sample. searchChooseIDFStatistics bundles all inputs into searchIDFInputs and calls it, making the choice a testable value rather than an ordering of statements."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "Integration into scoreSearchCandidates", "description": "The call to scoreSearchCandidates now goes through searchChooseIDFStatistics rather than passing fileDF and a simple selected-file count directly. The idfObserver hook fires immediately after, letting tests capture the exact DF map and file count each search scored with."}], "page_number": 1}, {"title": "One definition of document frequency across all backends", "summary": "fileDF previously mixed content and path matches, so cold (content scan) and warm (Git tree grep) backends computed different document frequencies for the same query. A new contentDF map counts only content matches, matching the definition preselection's posting totals follow and making the two backends agree.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "contentDF separates content from path matches", "description": "contentDF is populated alongside fileDF in the per-file scoring loop, counting only content matches. searchChooseIDFStatistics passes it as selectedContentDF when corpusFromSelected is true, giving every backend the same corpus DF definition and eliminating the prior cold/warm divergence."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "searchIDFInputs consolidates the decision inputs", "description": "A new struct gathers sampleDF, selectedInspected, selectedReadFailures, postingTotals, selectedContentDF, corpusFiles, and corpusFromSelected at one call site, separating the information-gathering pass from the selection logic."}], "page_number": 2}, {"title": "Preselection exactness tracking", "summary": "searchFileSelection gains corpusFiles and corpusFromSelected fields that record when a preselection pass can guarantee exact repository-wide document frequencies. Three paths claim exactness; unread files and the abbreviation-alias route revoke it.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "corpusFiles and corpusFromSelected fields on searchFileSelection", "description": "corpusFiles is the number of files in the searched tree when the selection can supply exact corpus DF; corpusFromSelected signals that counting over the selected files equals counting over the whole corpus. Three code paths set these: git grep without -I, all-files selection (IndexAllFiles or under MaxIndexedFiles), and a complete content scan with no read failures."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "Git grep switches to GrepTreePathsIncludingBinary", "description": "The plain fixed-string grep path changes from git grep -I to GrepTreePathsIncludingBinary (no -I flag). Files Git classifies as binary via -diff gitattributes or diff.default.binary=true config are now included in the preselected set, making it a genuine superset of every file containing a query term. NUL-containing files among them are still skipped by the scorer after inspection."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "Read failures and alias route revoke exactness", "description": "An unread selected file is UNKNOWN, not zero: its matches would be absent from DF while its path stays in N. selectedReadFailures tracks such files and a nonzero count zeros corpusFiles, forcing fallback to sample statistics. The abbreviation-alias route never claims exactness because it selects files by a different (validated alias) term set rather than the query terms themselves."}], "page_number": 3}, {"title": "Test infrastructure and coverage", "summary": "Two new test files add a per-search idfObserver hook, unit tests for the IDF selection logic, and integration tests with real Git repositories covering binary-attribute edge cases, read failures, and backend-invariance assertions.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/sem/search.go"], "title": "Per-search idfObserver test seam", "description": "SearchOptions gains an unexported idfObserver func(df map[string]int, files int, exact bool) field. It fires once per search with the exact statistics used, is scoped per-search so concurrent tests cannot capture each other's calls, and is nil in production."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search_corpus_idf_test.go"], "title": "Unit and integration tests for IDF selection logic", "description": "search_corpus_idf_test.go covers searchCorpusIDFStatistics (corpus preference over sample, sample fallback without a whole-corpus pass, corpus terms absent from the sample), searchChooseIDFStatistics (four literal cases from peer review), and end-to-end wiring through SearchRepository including unreadable-file exactness revocation and bounded-read scenarios."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search_corpus_idf_git_test.go"], "title": "Git-backend IDF invariance integration tests", "description": "search_corpus_idf_git_test.go builds real committed Git repositories and asserts that cold (content pass) and warm (git-tree-grep) backends score with identical IDF statistics. Cases cover -diff gitattribute, diff.default.binary=true config, NUL-containing files (counted in N but not DF), and the alias route which must not claim exactness."}], "page_number": 4}], "title": "Corpus-wide BM25 IDF for backend-invariant search ranking", "summary": "Replaces per-sample document frequencies in BM25 IDF with exact repository-wide counts when preselection can supply them, fixing a sample-bias defect that made rare identifying terms underweight and produced different rankings on cold vs warm backends. Also unifies the document-frequency definition across all search backends so cold and warm paths score identically."}
trail-summary {"markdown": "**Problem:**

BM25 scoring in the search API was computing inverse document frequency (IDF) from the preselected sample of files, which is inherently biased because preselection deliberately chooses files FOR containing the query words. This makes every query term appear common, reducing the discriminative power of rare, identifying terms and causing inconsistent scores across different preselection backends (Git tree grep vs. content pass).

**Solution:**

When preselection can prove it has examined the entire repository corpus, the search now uses repository-wide document frequencies for BM25 IDF calculation instead of sample statistics. This ensures:

1. **Backend consistency**: cold content passes and warm Git tree grep now score the same query identically, as they compute IDF from the same corpus-wide frequencies.

2. **Semantic precision**: rare repository-wide terms keep their high IDF weight because they're scored against all files, not just the biased sample.

3. **Correctness under constraints**: when preselection cannot guarantee full coverage (Git-narrowed or bounded selections), the search conservatively falls back to sample statistics rather than guessing.

The change also fixes a bug where `git grep -I` was excluding NUL-free text files marked with `.gitattributes -diff`, causing divergence between backends in the exact-statistics case. The preselection now calls `GrepTreePathsIncludingBinary` to capture every file matching a query term, properly detecting when corpus statistics are exact."}

