{"chunk_id":"8c3cf3","wall_time_seconds":2.313930833,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":2637,"output":"trail-confidence {"automation": "trail-confidence", "resultType": "trail_monitor", "data": {"value": 74, "rationale": "The opt-in semantic channel has extensive behavioural tests: byte-identical output when it is off or fails, loopback-only and proxy/redirect-free networking, no requests under --no-network, cancellation that never saves a partial cache, and isolated stub servers. Confidence is held back by the size and churn of the 1388-line ranking and file-selection logic, thin ranking-quality coverage, and Linux-only test CI.", "monitor_key": "confidence"}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T13:55:06.879Z"}
trail-drift {"automation": "trail-drift", "resultType": "trail_monitor", "data": {"value": 58, "rationale": "The CLI, schema and cache changes follow existing patterns and are additive, off by default and fall back cleanly. But the branch adds an HTTP model-call path to the provider and rewrites the documented no-network/no-model rule as a default, and the channel adds special cases across many search passes.", "monitor_key": "drift"}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T13:54:59.517Z"}
trail-review {"automation": "trail-review", "resultType": "code_review_comments", "data": {"summary": "", "comments": []}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T13:54:34.144Z"}
trail-risk {"automation": "trail-risk", "resultType": "trail_monitor", "data": {"value": 72, "rationale": "The branch adds about 5.8k lines, centered on the new `internal/sem/search_semantic.go` (1,388 lines). It is the provider's first network path: when enabled, it sends signatures, doc comments and query text to an embedding service that must be on localhost/loopback. The client ignores proxies, refuses redirects and never does a DNS lookup, and the channel is off unless two env vars are set, with `--no-network` turning it off for queries. It also changes the central query ranking path (`search.go`, related/contract/multi-resolution), the cache commit path (`cache_entry.go`) and the `index`/`query` CLI, and adds new JSON output fields, so the no-egress contract, ranking, CLI and output-schema risk all apply even though output without the channel is documented as byte-identical.", "monitor_key": "risk"}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T13:54:38.584Z"}
trail-semantic-diff {"automation": "trail-semantic-diff", "resultType": "trail_semantic_diff", "data": {"pages": [{"title": "Configuration and CLI surface", "summary": "Both environment variables and the index flag turn the channel on, and a per-call flag turns it off. The default payload stays unchanged.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/cli/env.go", "internal/cli/index.go", "internal/cli/help.go"], "title": "Env gating and index --semantic", "description": "The channel is configured only when both ENTIRE_GRAPH_SEMANTIC_ENDPOINT and ENTIRE_GRAPH_SEMANTIC_MODEL are set. `index` gains `--semantic`, which builds the semantic index for the committed tree and reports it in an additive `semantic` field and a text line. It fails fast, before the snapshot build, if the configuration is missing or `--no-network` is passed. `--no-network` is now recorded rather than ignored."}, {"files": ["internal/cli/search.go", "internal/cli/search_result_json.go", "internal/cli/search_session.go"], "title": "Query flags, rendering and replay identity", "description": "`query` gains `--no-semantic`. The text and agent renderers show the channel state (`sem=<status>`) only when it is configured. Rows the channel synthesized print `semantic_score` instead of a misleading `score=0`. The session replay scope now includes a hashed semantic identity, so a stored answer is never replayed under a different channel configuration. Unconfigured output is meant to be byte-identical to before."}]}, {"title": "Semantic engine and ranking", "summary": "The new engine embeds queries against a per-tree index and fuses the results with the lexical ranking without disturbing it. It fails open on any error.", "sections": [{"files": ["internal/sem/search_semantic.go", "internal/sem/search_semantic_decode.go", "internal/sem/search_semantic_json.go", "internal/sem/cache_entry.go"], "title": "Semantic index and embedding client", "description": "`search_semantic.go` adds the whole engine: a loopback-only HTTP embedder client and an index keyed by tree, model and recipe version. The index is bound to the corpus it embedded. Vectors that overflow or are not unit length are rejected. Diagnostics omit URLs and model names. Caller cancellation propagates through the channel. Any channel failure reports a status (`used`, `off:flag`, `off:worktree` or `unavailable:<reason>`) and returns the lexical answer. The channel never runs in worktree mode. The index cache version was bumped to v2."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search.go", "internal/sem/search_confidence.go", "internal/sem/search_contract.go", "internal/sem/search_enclosure.go", "internal/sem/search_multi_resolution.go", "internal/sem/search_related.go", "internal/sem/search_span_merge.go"], "title": "Fusion, nomination and lexical protection", "description": "Semantic and lexical results are interleaved, embedding first, and deduplicated by symbol ID. On a warm index, nominated files only add symbols after the lexical ranking is final. On a cold index they are counted against `--max-indexed-files`, and the lexical tail can be evicted, but never the lexical head. The channel never changes lexical scores or idf, and it does not change the fix-site promotion or the prose expansion order. Nominations only come from files already in the corpus, so ignore rules still apply. Provenance (`semantic:only`, `semantic:embedding`) is recorded explicitly. The search pipeline, confidence, enclosure, related and span-merge code were adjusted to fit this."}]}, {"title": "Contract documentation and tests", "summary": "The docs state the exception to the no-egress contract. Many regression suites pin the safety and invariance properties.", "sections": [{"files": ["docs/search.md", "docs/trust-and-security.md", "README.md", "AGENTS.md"], "title": "Docs and security contract", "description": "The docs describe the payload fields (`semantic_score`, `stats.semantic_*`) and the loopback-only exception to the no-network default. They state what data is sent to the embedder: the query, symbol names, signatures and up to 40 doc lines. They also say that `--no-network` disables the channel and that `format_version` stays 1."}, {"files": ["internal/sem/search_semantic_test.go", "internal/sem/search_semantic_lexical_invariance_test.go", "internal/sem/search_semantic_cancellation_regression_test.go", "internal/cli/search_semantic_test.go", "internal/cli/search_session_semantic_test.go"], "title": "Regression coverage", "description": "About 4k lines of tests cover the following areas. Several come from adversarial re-reviews.\n- the environment gate and score clamp\n- lexical-ranking invariance and head protection\n- cancellation and nomination-snapshot failure\n- corpus-bounded nomination\n- diagnostic redaction\n- JSON and decode shape\n- session replay scoping\n- unchanged output when the channel is unconfigured"}]}], "title": "Opt-in semantic retrieval channel for query", "summary": "Adds an opt-in, loopback-only embedding channel. `entire graph index --semantic` builds a tree-keyed vector index, and `query --head` fuses embedding hits with the lexical ranking. It does nothing unless configured, and it fails open to the lexical answer. Most of the branch is hardening fixes and regression tests."}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T13:55:45.373Z"}
trail-summary {"automation": "trail-summary", "resultType": "trail_summary", "data": {"markdown": "**Problem:** Agent-phrased queries often miss the right function when the function's name and body share no words with the question. Lexical ranking alone cannot bridge that gap. The branch adds an opt-in semantic retrieval channel to `entire graph query`, which uses local embeddings to find functions that lexical search misses.\n\n**Solution:** The channel is off unless the user sets both `ENTIRE_GRAPH_SEMANTIC_ENDPOINT` (a localhost or loopback URL) and `ENTIRE_GRAPH_SEMANTIC_MODEL`. It also requires `--head`. Users build a vector index with `entire graph index --semantic`, which embeds each function from one committed tree. At query time, the channel's nearest functions are interleaved with the lexical ranking, embedding first, and deduplicated by symbol ID. Any channel failure falls open to the unchanged lexical answer with a warning.\n\n- **CLI:** `index` gains `--semantic`, which cannot be combined with `--no-network`. `query` gains `--no-semantic` to skip the channel for one call and `--no-network` to disable it. The index JSON gets a `semantic` object only when `--semantic` is passed.\n- **Payload:** Configured results may include `semantic_score` and the `semantic:embedding` or `semantic:only` signals. The `stats` block reports `semantic_status` and related counters. Unconfigured output is meant to be byte-identical to before. `format_version` stays at 1 because all new fields are additive.\n- **Contract:** The default no-egress guarantee still holds. The semantic channel is a documented exception that sends queries, function names, signatures, and up to 40 doc-comment lines to the configured loopback service. The README, AGENTS.md, `docs/search.md`, and the trust/security doc were updated to match.\n- **Cache:** The semantic index is keyed by tree, model, and recipe version, and its cache version is now v2.\n- **Safety fixes:** The branch includes many review-driven fixes. These cover loopback-only endpoints, rejecting oversized or non-unit vectors, cancellation propagation, binding the index to its corpus, and protecting lexical top rows from displacement after fusion. Cache writes can now be vetoed just before commit, so a cancelled build keeps the old entry.\n- **Parser and identity:** The diff does not touch parser code, `compound-v1` symbol IDs, or `IdentityRevision`.\n- **Performance:** On a warm index, the channel only adds nominated files and leaves lexical scores unchanged. On a cold search, nominations count against `--max-indexed-files`, which can evict lexical tail files, so surviving scores may shift slightly. Results are no longer guaranteed to be sorted by `score`. The branch has no benchmark artifacts beyond a commit message mentioning benchmark parity.\n\nScope: 40 files changed, about 5,834 insertions and 43 deletions, mostly in `internal/sem/search_semantic.go` (about 1,390 lines) and its tests."}, "createdAt": "2026-10-17T13:55:44.464Z"}
"}

